<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kabuki">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kabuki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kabuki is a form of Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> combining drama, music and dance, known for stylised acting and elaborate costumes. It developed in the early seventeenth century and flourished in the urban culture of Edo. It is among the cultural legacies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kabuki.jpg" alt="Kabuki" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morita-za_in_the_1790s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The history of kabuki is traced to 1603, when Izumo no Okuni, a former shrine maiden who may have served at Izumo-taisha, began performing with a troupe of young women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. They gave a simple style of dance drama in pantomime on a makeshift stage in the dry bed of the Kamo River. In the earliest forms women played both men and women in comic playlets about ordinary life. Okuni was asked to perform before the Imperial Court, and rival troupes quickly formed. </p><p>The word is believed to derive from the verb kabuku, meaning to lean or to be out of the ordinary; the kabukimono were originally people bizarrely dressed, such as gangs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>. The characters for sing, dance and skill are ateji and do not reflect the etymology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>After women and young boys were banned from the stage in 1629, kabuki became the all-male form called yarō-kabuki. Male actors known as onnagata took over the women's roles, with young men preferred for their less obviously masculine appearance and higher voices. A kabuki stage has the hanamichi, or flower path, a walkway through the audience used for entrances and exits. Revolving stages and trap doors were introduced in the 18th century to produce sudden revelations and transformations. </p><p>Actors wear layered kimono in vibrant colours and the white oshiroi base. Kumadori make-up exaggerates facial lines into masks, with red for passion and heroism, blue or black for villainy, green for the supernatural and purple for nobility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the Genroku period the structure of the plays took the form still performed, and kabuki and the puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> ningyō jōruri, later called bunraku, influenced each other. Chikamatsu Monzaemon was among the first professional kabuki playwrights; his The Love Suicides at Sonezaki, written for bunraku and adapted for kabuki, reportedly inspired copycat suicides and led to a government ban on plays about love suicides in 1723. The actor Ichikawa Danjūrō I is credited with the mie style of posing.  In 1842, under the Tenpō Reforms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> forced three theatres out of Edo into Asakusa and restricted the trade and socialising that went on in them.</p><p>After 1868 actors and playwrights worked to improve the reputation of kabuki, and the Emperor sponsored a performance on 21 April 1887. The occupying forces banned it briefly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, but the ban was rescinded by 1947. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed it on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kabuki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The arts flourished under the Tokugawa peace: <b>kabuki</b> and bunraku puppet theatre, the ukiyo-e woodblock prints of artists such as Hokusai and Hiroshige, the hokku verse, ancestor of the haiku, that Matsuo Basho raised to a high art, and an urban popular culture centred on the chonin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
